// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/OWNERSHIP.md R10 (an `arc` value may not be made unique),
// reached through a CALL RESULT rather than through a binding annotation.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This program was a LIVE double free, not a masked one.
// Measured end to end against the compiler before the fix:
//
//     cell check                                    exit 0
//     cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fsanitize=address   exit 0
//     running it                                    exit 134
//
//     AddressSanitizer: attempting double-free
//       cell_slice_free <- cell_slice_drop_glue <- cell_arc_drop <- cell_main
//
// `cell_take` frees the buffer with `cell_slice_free(&mine)`, and then
// `cell_arc_drop(_cell_t4)` on the caller's unbound temporary frees the same
// buffer again through the box's drop glue. `cell_arc_clone` governs a
// refcount and the buffer is not what the refcount governs, so no retain can
// fix it: R10 refuses instead.
//
// WHY IT ESCAPED, which is the part worth keeping. R10's refusal had already
// been widened once, from four PLACE positions to asking about the whole
// EXPRESSION, which closed the value-shape axis (a `match` or a block tail
// yielding an `arc` place). That widening is correct and it does not reach
// this program. Here the `arc`-ness comes from `fresh`'s declared RETURN TYPE,
// so there is no place to interrogate and no expression shape to look through.
// Three separate axes cross at an `owned` position and only one had been
// closed:
//
//     expression shape    place versus value          closed by 23353e9
//     arc source          annotation versus signature closed here
//     consumption site    four positions versus all   closed here
//
// THE UNMASKING, measured rather than reasoned. `23353e9`'s commit message
// says its fix "lands before any such change and not after", meaning before
// the unbound-temporary release that would turn a leak into a double free.
// That is false for this program. Emitting it at `1aacf5d` produces
// `cell_take((*(const cell_slice_t *)cell_fresh().ptr))` with no drop at all,
// a leak; emitting it at `460b9a3` already produces the hoisted temporary with
// `cell_arc_drop(_cell_t4)`. So the mask came off in `460b9a3`, seven commits
// BEFORE the fix that claimed to be preempting it, and this was a live double
// free for that whole stretch. (The axis-1 `match` case that `23353e9` did fix
// was genuinely still masked at `23353e9^`: its value temporary is cloned and
// never dropped. Two different programs, two different answers, which is why
// the ordering claim had to be measured per program rather than asserted.)
//
// The rule is now asked at SIX consumption sites, not four: an `owned`
// parameter, an `owned` binding, an assignment into an `owned` place, an
// `owned` struct field, a list-literal element, and a `return` whose declared
// return type is not `arc`. And the classifier's verdict is total: a source
// whose ownership cannot be resolved is refused rather than permitted, because
// permitting by default is what let each of these three axes through in turn.
//
// A sanitizer stage cannot see this file, and that is not a gap: a refused
// program emits no C, so there is nothing to run. What pins it is the rejected
// loop in tools/check.sh reading the EXPECT line above. The ASan stage's job
// is the programs that are still accepted; see examples/arc_return_field.cell.
//
// Everything that stays legal is unchanged: `fresh` below returns its own
// `arc` local through an `arc` return type, which is the arc-to-arc case, and
// `take(owned <a plain owned place>)` is still accepted. Only making an `arc`
// value UNIQUE is refused. See examples/rejected/arc_to_owned.cell for the
// same rule reached through a binding annotation.

pub fn fresh() -> arc [Int] {
    let arc xs: [Int] = [1, 2, 3]
    return xs
}

pub fn take(owned xs: [Int]) -> Int {
    let owned mine: [Int] = xs
    return 7
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    let copy n = take(owned fresh())
    print_int(n)
}
